  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general business law, in relation to increasing fines for selling and manufacturing fur-bearing articles of clothing",
    "basePrintNo": "S7815",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7815-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §399-aaa, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7815\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the general business law, in relation to increasing\nfines for selling and manufacturing fur-bearing articles of clothing\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to ensure that clothing stores comply with\nthe current labeling requirements for fur and faux fur articles of\nclothing, by stiffening penalties.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one amends subdivision 4 of section 399-aaa of the general\nbusiness law, as added by chapter 611 of the laws of 2037 that\nprohibited any person, firm, partnership or corporation from knowingly\nimporting for profit, selling at retail, offering for sale at retail,\nor manufacturing articles of clothing which include, or have attached\nin any quantity and of any value, fur that is not labeled as being\nfaux fur or, real fur, or is incorrectly labeled.\n\nSection two sets forth the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nIn 2007, the legislation passed a law requiring that articles of\nclothing that contained fur or faux fur be labeled as such. At the\ntime, some stores were using inaccurate labels, and undercover\ninvestigations found that clothing that was labeled as faux fur was\nactually authentic animal fur, often raccoon dog fur.\n\nIn the course of follow-up investigations, it was found that stores\nare continuing this practice. This legislation would increase the\npenalties associated with violations of this statute to discourage\nthis sustained non-compliance.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2016: A.1047 Passed the Assembly on 5/9/16\n2015: A.1047 Reported to Codes on 5/12/2015.\n2014: A.6936-A Amended and Recommitted to Codes on 5/5/14. Advanced to\nThird Reading Cal. 691 on 5/15/14.\n2013: A.6936 Referred to Consumer Affairs and Protection\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\n\nNone to the State.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
